// Jest Snapshot v1, https://goo.gl/fbAQLP

exports[`buildJudgeSystemPrompt grades a related repo finding by its kind, only when the finders could read related repos 1`] = `
"## Related Repos

Reviewers can read these repos with their tools. References, which this repository implements or ports: \`acme/spec\`. Consumers, which depend on this repository: \`acme/app\`.

A finding cites their code as \`owner/name:path:line\`. Its tool evidence says whether each cited line is in the checkout the reviewers read. A citation listed there as in the fetched checkout is evidence the reviewer read, so it counts as code you can see. Manki shows the lines the fetched checkout holds at a citation under the finding as **Cited code in a related repo**, when it could read them. It shows them for every citation, also one the tool evidence lists as holding only part of the quoted code or none of it, and one the tool evidence does not list. For such a citation the shown lines are what is really at that place, and the citation itself proves nothing: grade from what the shown lines hold, never from what the finding says they hold. A citation that is neither listed as in the fetched checkout nor shown rests on code you cannot see.

- **A divergence from a reference** is graded by the blocker tests and the severity table, like any other finding.
- **A change that breaks a consumer** is the \`degraded\` row in the \`normal\` column, a \`warning\` that lists the consumer's call sites and says it breaks once the consumer upgrades. It is the \`severe\` row, a \`blocker\`, only when the PR description says the change does not break anything. This replaces the broken public contract part of the \`severe\` row for a consumer.

"
`;
